    Pass in the card reader, not the answer
    
    Instead of handing buyCoffee() the bank's answer, hand it the thing
    that gives answers. buyCoffee() keeps its job, charging 450 cents and
    reacting, and whoever calls it decides which card reader it uses.
    
    That needs a type for "a card reader", so cart.ts declares CardReader,
    with its contract: true if charged, false if declined, never throws.
    BankCardReader implements it. Before, cart.ts imported bank.ts: the cart
    depended on the bank. Now bank.ts imports cart.ts. The cart says what it
    needs, and the bank provides it. That is the dependency inversion
    principle. main.ts is the one place that picks the real bank.
    
    The tests hand buyCoffee() stand-ins for the bank, test doubles that
    always give the same answer, so each test picks the outcome it needs.
    Both tests pass every run.

